This information sheet describes, in general terms, California's Solar Shade Control Act, Public Resources Code §§ 25980–25986, in connection with the solar collector described below and located at {{party1PropertyAddress}} (owned or controlled by {{party1Name}}), and the tree or shrub described below and located at {{party2PropertyAddress}} (owned or controlled by {{party2Name}}).

Solar collector at issue: {{solarCollectorDescription}}

Tree or shrub at issue: {{treeShrubDescription}}

The Solar Shade Control Act addresses shading of a solar collector by a tree or shrub on another person's property, planted or grown after the collector's installation. It covers the statute's own name and legislative policy statement; its definition of "solar collector" and the siting rules a collector itself must meet; the statutory 10-percent shading restriction and the hours to which it applies; the private-nuisance status a violating tree or shrub can acquire, and the written-notice step the statute makes a prerequisite to that status; the statute's own listed exemptions; the authority a city or county has to opt out of, or supersede, the statute locally; and a separate statutory exemption procedure for a passive or natural solar system impacted by another, active solar system. This topic is distinct from Civil Code §§ 714 and 714.1 (the California Solar Rights Act, addressed in this corpus's own information sheet), which void certain restrictions on installing or using a solar energy system in the first place, and from Civil Code § 841 (the Good Neighbor Fence Act, also addressed in this corpus), which concerns cost-sharing for a boundary fence between adjoining landowners, not shading of a solar collector by vegetation. This sheet is informational only. It does not itself measure any shadow, determine whether any specific tree or shrub violates Public Resources Code § 25982, determine whether any exemption in § 25984 applies, determine whether {{party1PropertyAddress}} or {{party2PropertyAddress}} lies within a city or county that has adopted an ordinance under § 25985, or resolve any actual dispute between {{party1Name}} and {{party2Name}}.

WRITTEN NOTICE REGARDING PUBLIC RESOURCES CODE SECTION 25982

Date of this notice: {{noticeDate}}

From: {{party1Name}}, owner or person in control of the solar collector at {{party1PropertyAddress}}
To: {{party2Name}}, owner or person in control of the property at {{party2PropertyAddress}}

Solar collector at issue: {{solarCollectorDescription}}

Tree or shrub at issue: {{treeShrubDescription}}

This notice references Public Resources Code § 25982, which restricts a tree or shrub from casting a shadow greater than 10 percent of a solar collector's absorption area upon the collector surface at any one time between 10 a.m. and 2 p.m., local standard time, and Public Resources Code § 25983, under which a tree or shrub maintained in violation of Section 25982 is a private nuisance if it is not removed or altered after a written notice such as this one requesting compliance with Section 25982.

This notice states facts about Public Resources Code §§ 25982 and 25983; it does not itself determine that {{treeShrubDescription}} in fact violates Section 25982, does not itself determine that any exemption under Section 25984 or any local ordinance under Section 25985 does not apply, and does not itself determine that a private nuisance under Section 25983 has in fact arisen.

